.th REGEN VIII 5/31/77
.sh NAME
regen \*- regenerate system directories
.sh DESCRIPTION
There are several system directories that contain
various commands and archive libraries.
These include
.it "/bin, /usr/bin, /lib,"
.it "/usr/lib, /etc, /usr/hasp,"
and
.it "/usr/fort."
To facilitate the regeneration of these
directories, the
.it make\c
(I)
command is used.
Each directory contains the
.it make
description files used to build, update, or regenerate
the directory.
If one description file suffices, it is named
.it \fB.\fPmakefile;
otherwise the files are named
.it "\fB.\fPmakefile1, \fB.\fPmakefile2, ..."
.s3
For best results, you should be logged in as ``root'' (or as super-user).
.s3
For example, the following
will recompile any
.it /bin
commands
that are out-of-date with respect to their corresponding source files:
.in +5n
.nf
.s3
chdir /bin
make \*-f \fB.\fPmakefile1
make \*-f \fB.\fPmakefile2
make \*-f \fB.\fPmakefile3
.s3
.fi
.in -5n
As another example, the following forces the recompilation
of any
.it /bin
commands that call
.it ctime\c
(III),
in addition to recompiling anything that is out-of-date in
.it /bin:
.in +5n
.nf
.s3
chdir /bin
make \*-f \fB.\fPmakefile1 CTIME=RC
make \*-f \fB.\fPmakefile2 CTIME=RC
make \*-f \fB.\fPmakefile3 CTIME=RC
.s3
.fi
.in -5n
The section on ``Time Zones'' below
explains why this is useful.
Similar arguments force recompilation of anything that uses the C compiler (CCDEP=RC),
the assembler (ASDEP=RC),
the
.it yacc\c
(I) processor (YACCDEP=RC),
the ``\*-lS'' library (LSDEP=RC),
the ``\*-lPW'' library (LPWDEP=RC),
the ``\*-lpw'' library (LpwDEP=RC),
and the UNIX operating system ``include'' files (SYSDEP=RC).
.if t .sp -.5v
.sh "Time Zones:"
Commands such as
.it ls\c
(I)
and
.it date\c
(I)
give Eastern times (EST or EDT).
This has been known to annoy
non-East Coast (USA) and non-USA users.
To get your local time,
you must change
.it ctime\c
(III),
which converts the GMT
.it time\c
(II)
time-stamps to local time,
and then regenerate all commands that use
.it ctime.
To be precise, you must:
.s3
.in +\w'\(em\ 'u
.ti -\w'\(em\ 'u
\(em\ Change
.it ctime\c
(III).
The source currently lives in
.it /sys/source/s4/ctime.c,
and the object resides in the standard C library,
.it /lib/libc.a.
.s3
.ti -\w'\(em\ 'u
\(em\ Regenerate the standard C library, as follows:
.s3
.in +5n
.nf
chdir /lib
make \*-f \fB.\fPmakefile1
make \*-f \fB.\fPmakefile2
make \*-f \fB.\fPmakefile3
make \*-f \fB.\fPmakefile4
.fi
.in -5n
.s3
This works because,
as a result of the change to the source for
.it ctime\c
(III),
.it /lib/libc.a
is now out-of-date with respect to
its source,
namely the
.it ctime
part.
.s3
.ti -\w'\(em\ 'u
\(em\ Regenerate all directories,
using the CTIME=RC option on the
.it make
command line:
.s3
.in +5n
.nf
chdir /bin
make \*-f \fB.\fPmakefile1 CTIME=RC
make \*-f \fB.\fPmakefile2 CTIME=RC
make \*-f \fB.\fPmakefile3 CTIME=RC
chdir /usr/bin
make \*-f \fB.\fPmakefile1 CTIME=RC
make \*-f \fB.\fPmakefile2 CTIME=RC
make \*-f \fB.\fPmakefile3 CTIME=RC
make \*-f \fB.\fPmakefile4 CTIME=RC
chdir /lib
make \*-f \fB.\fPmakefile1 CTIME=RC
make \*-f \fB.\fPmakefile2 CTIME=RC
make \*-f \fB.\fPmakefile3 CTIME=RC
make \*-f \fB.\fPmakefile4 CTIME=RC
chdir /usr/lib
make \*-f \fB.\fPmakefile1 CTIME=RC
make \*-f \fB.\fPmakefile2 CTIME=RC
chdir /etc
make \*-f \fB.\fPmakefile CTIME=RC
chdir /usr/hasp
make \*-f \fB.\fPmakefile CTIME=RC
chdir /usr/fort
make \*-f \fB.\fPmakefile CTIME=RC
.fi
.in -5n
.in -\w'\(em\ 'u
.s3
The full directory list (as of this moment!)
is given above for completeness.
Some directories might not have anything that uses
.it ctime.
.s3
Of course, you should test your new
.it ctime
before installing it in the standard C library.
See also the ``BUGS'' section below.
.if t .sp -.5v
.sh "/bin:"
Regeneration of
.it /bin
is straightforward,
except for
.it as\c
(I),
.it ld\c
(I),
and
.it cc\c
(I).
The
.it make
description files for
.it /bin
do
.it not
automatically regenerate
.it cc;
instead, a separate procedure is used (see ``cc'' below).
.s3
.it As
and
.it ld
are necessary to regenerate themselves.
That is, one cannot re-assemble
.it as
unless a working
.it as
already exists.
Thus, if something goes wrong and a bad
.it as
or
.it ld
is generated, you'll have to retrieve a working version from a backup tape.
.s3
The moral:
before regenerating
.it /bin,
be sure that you either have a good backup tape,
or else have emergency copies of
.it as
and
.it ld
somewhere.
.if t .sp -.5v
.sh "/usr/bin:"
\&\fB.\fPmakefile1 and \fB.\fPmakefile2 regenerate the basic
.it /usr/bin
commands;
\&\fB.\fPmakefile3 regenerates the SCCS commands;
\&\fB.\fPmakefile4 regenerates
.it eqn\c
(I) and
.it troff\c
(I).
If you do not have the PWB/UNIX
.it troff\c
(I) and
.it eqn\c
(I) package,
\&\fB.\fPmakefile4 will fail.
.if t .sp -.5v
.sh "/lib:"
As with
.it as
and
.it ld
in
.it /bin,
.it as2
in
.it /lib
(``pass\ 2'' of
.it as\c
)
can't be regenerated if it doesn't already exist.
Also,
.it c0,
.it c1,
.it c2,
and
.it cpp
in
.it /lib
are parts of the C compiler,
and the
.it make
description files for
.it /lib
do not automatically regenerate them
(see ``cc'' below).
.if t .sp -.5v
.sh "/usr/lib:"
\&\fB.\fPmakefile1 regenerates the basic
.it /usr/lib
modules;
\&\fB.\fPmakefile2 regenerates tables used by
.it eqn\c
(I) and
.it troff\c
(I).
If you do not have the PWB/UNIX
.it troff\c
(I) and
.it eqn\c
(I) package,
\&\fB.\fPmakefile2 will fail.
.if t .sp -.5v
.sh "cc:"
.it /bin/cc
is just the tip of the C compiler iceberg.
The real work is done by
.it "c0, c1, c2,"
and
.it cpp
in
.it /lib.
Since these programs work together,
they must be updated carefully.
Furthermore, since the C compiler is written in C,
if you install a bad C compiler
you'll have to retrieve a working version from a backup tape.
.s3
Therefore, the
.it make
procedures for
.it /bin
and
.it /lib
do not attempt to regenerate the C compiler.
Instead, you must do it explicitly, by:
.s3
.in +5n
.nf
chdir /sys/c/c
make \*-f makefile install clean
.fi
.in -5n
.s3
That creates
.it "cc, c0, c1, c2,"
and
.it cpp
in
.it /sys/c/c,
and, if no errors occur,
moves them to
.it /bin
and
.it /lib.
``Clean'' just removes any object files
(\fB.\fP\fIo\fP)
left around.
Note that the
.it make
description file for the C compiler is
.it makefile
(no leading `\fB.\fP').
Only the
.it make
description files that live in object directories
(such as
.it /bin\c
)
start with `\fB.\fP'.
.s3
Often it is better to install a new version of the C compiler as
.it ncc,
so that it can be tested without destroying the old
(working) version.
The following creates and installs
a complete new version of the C compiler as
.it ncc:
.s3
.in +5n
.nf
chdir /sys/c/c
make \*-f makefile PREF=n install clean
.fi
.in -5n
.s3
As before, this creates
.it "cc, c0, c1, c2,"
and
.it "cpp"
in
.it /sys/c/c.
But they are installed as
.it "ncc, nc0, nc1, nc2,"
and
.it ncpp
in
.it /bin
and
.it /lib.
This works because the
.it cc
driver
looks at the name under which it was invoked.
If the first character of the name is `c',
it calls
.it c0,
etc., in
.it /lib.
But if the first character isn't `c',
it prepends that character to the names of the load modules
that it calls.
Thus
.it ncc
calls
.it "nc0, nc1, nc2,"
and
.it ncpp
in
.it /lib.
.if t .sp -.5v
.sh "/usr/hasp:"
The
.it \fB.\fPmakefile
for
.it /usr/hasp
specifies whether your RJE system
supports one or two lines.
The following generates a two-line system:
.in +5n
.nf
.s3
chdir /usr/hasp
make \*-f \fB.\fPmakefile NHASP=2
.s3
.fi
.in -5n
while:
.in +5n
.nf
.s3
chdir /usr/hasp
make \*-f \fB.\fPmakefile NHASP=
.s3
.fi
.in -5n
generates a one-line system
(the default is one-line).
To change the default, change the ``NHASP='' line
near the beginning of the
.it \fB.\fPmakefile.
.sh "SEE ALSO"
make(I)
.br
.it "Make \*- A Program for Maintaining Computer Programs"
by S. I. Feldman
.sh "DIAGNOSTICS"
``Don\*at know how to make xxx'':	The source file xxx doesn't exist.
.sh FILES
\&\fB.\fPmakefile*
.sh BUGS
Not every directory has a \fB.\fPmakefile.
.s3
.it Nroff\c
(I)
and
.it troff\c
(I)
are fixed to Eastern Standard Time;
they use their own conversion routines, instead of calling
.it ctime\c
(III).
Fortunately, they use just the date,
not the time-of-day.
